@0 = ~Urkodoël~
@99999 = ~This component requires BG2EE or EET.~
//Base: no extra hit points
@1 = ~Ah, a visitor! Welcome, mortal. I am Urkodoël, an anarchilich, and a purveyor of knowledge that certain gods would rather see forgotten. If you are a cleric, druid, or shaman, perhaps we have some business to discuss.~

@2 = ~And warriors, thieves, or mages? Are you not interested in them?~

@3 = ~Forbidden knowledge? That deserves some explanation.~

@4 = ~Not in the slightest. Warriors have their weapons, thieves their tricks, and mages their spellbooks. I see no reason to offer them what they already possess. My specialty is divine spellcasters.~

@5 = ~Very well. What exactly do you offer?~

@6 = ~Oh, hardly anything. I can teach a cleric the spells of an archmage, a druid the secrets of arcane magic, or a shaman incantations no spirit would ever whisper to them. And all without giving up their divine magic! Better yet, every spell learned also grants an additional slot in which to memorize it. A minor bending of the rules of magic, you might say. The gods hate it. I love it.~

@7 = ~That sounds almost too good to be true. What is your price?~

@8 = ~Gold? How vulgar! No, I prefer a more... personal currency. For every spell level I teach you, I take one of your maximum hit points. Permanently, of course. A third-circle spell will cost you three hit points. Think of it as an investment in your education.~

@9 = ~That said, if you absolutely insist on getting rid of some gold, I also have a modest collection of items that might interest you. I am not entirely opposed to conventional commerce.~

@10 = ~Ah, you again! Have you come to broaden your knowledge? Or perhaps to shed a few years of life expectancy? Rest assured, I make no distinction between the two.~

@11 = ~Show me the spells you can teach me.~

@12 = ~I would like to see the items you sell.~

@13 = ~Remind me of the terms of our little arrangement.~

@14 = ~Not today, thank you.~

//If the character is neither a cleric, a druid, nor a shaman:

@15 = ~Well, look who is back! Still no aptitude for divine magic, I see. You know, visiting me again will do nothing to remedy that unfortunate situation. But if you enjoy my company so much, I can spare you a few moments. I make no promises that they will be pleasant.~

@16 = ~Are you certain there is nothing you can do for me?~

@17 = ~Remind me why you cannot teach me anything.~

@18 = ~In that case, I shall leave you to your business.~

//Reply to @13 :
@19 = ~With pleasure! I impart knowledge the gods certainly never intended you to possess. In exchange, you surrender one maximum hit point per spell level. A permanent payment, naturally. And each spell taught grants you an additional spell memorization slot. Is that not a wonderfully fair transaction?~

//Reply to @17 :
@20 = ~Because my art consists of fitting arcane magic into the little compartments the gods reserved for their faithful. Without those compartments, I have nothing to fill! And believe me, creating them from scratch would be a far more tiresome undertaking.~

//Reply to @16 :
@21 = ~Absolutely certain. My teachings are reserved for clerics, druids, and shamans, and my wares are intended exclusively for them. You could certainly buy something, but you would be utterly unable to use it. And I despise customers who come back to complain.~

@30 = ~What level of spell would you like to learn?~
@31 = ~I would like to learn a level 1 spell.~
@32 = ~I would like to learn a level 2 spell.~
@33 = ~I would like to learn a level 3 spell.~
@34 = ~I would like to learn a level 4 spell.~
@35 = ~I would like to learn a level 5 spell.~
@36 = ~I would like to learn a level 6 spell.~
@37 = ~I would like to learn a level 7 spell.~
@38 = ~I would like to learn a level 8 spell.~
@39 = ~I would like to learn a level 9 spell.~
@3999 = ~I would like to learn an epic spell.~
@40 = ~I will be back.~
@41 = ~I made a mistake. I would like to choose another spell level.~~I made a mistake. I would like to choose another spell level.~

@50 = ~Epic spells! Here we stand beyond the limits that even the gods claim to impose. What delicious insolence! Let us see what I can teach you.~

@51 = ~The first circle! One must start somewhere. Even the greatest heresies began with a small transgression.~

@52 = ~The second circle, very well. The gods are starting to frown, and we are only getting started! Let us see what I have to offer.~

@53 = ~The third circle? An excellent choice! Let us see what knowledge you are willing to pay for with your own flesh...~

@54 = ~The fourth circle! We are entering realms where the divine authorities begin to grow seriously concerned. How delightful!~

@55 = ~The fifth circle! Now things are getting interesting. At this point, a few orthodox priests would already be preparing your excommunication.~

@56 = ~The sixth circle! At this level, even the gods must be wondering who gave you permission. Certainly not me: I never ask permission.~

@57 = ~The seventh circle! The pinnacle of ordinary priestly magic. And to think some people claim clerics must make do with whatever their gods grant them!~

@58 = ~The eighth circle! Spells that many archmages will never master, and you will be able to cast them by invoking your deity. What irony!~

@59 = ~The ninth circle! We have reached the pinnacle of ordinary magic. "Ordinary," of course, is a word archmages seldom appreciate. Let us see what I have to offer.~

@60 = ~There we are! One more piece of knowledge in your mind, a little less vitality in your body. Ah, the joys of commerce! Can I be of further assistance?~

@61 = ~Yes, I would like to learn another spell.~

@62 = ~No, that will be enough for today. Farewell, Urkodoël.~
@63 = ~Oh, how unfortunate! Your reserves of vitality are simply insufficient to pay the price of this lesson. And unlike certain merchants, I do not extend credit. Come back when you have grown a little sturdier.~

@64 = ~Hmm... You seem somewhat worse for wear. I could certainly proceed with the lesson, but you might die before you could put it to use. That would be most regrettable for our business relationship! Go and get yourself healed, and we shall resume afterward.~

